NNAAMMEE
       wipefs - wipe a signature from a device

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       wwiippeeffss [options] _d_e_v_i_c_e...

       wwiippeeffss [----bbaacckkuupp] --oo _o_f_f_s_e_t _d_e_v_i_c_e...

       wwiippeeffss [----bbaacckkuupp] --aa _d_e_v_i_c_e...

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       wwiippeeffss can erase filesystem, raid or partition-table signatures (magic
       strings) from the specified _d_e_v_i_c_e to make the signatures invisible for
       libblkid. wwiippeeffss does not erase the filesystem itself nor any other
       data from the device.

       When used without any options, wwiippeeffss lists all visible filesystems and
       the offsets of their basic signatures. The default output is subject to
       change. So whenever possible, you should avoid using default outputs in
       your scripts. Always explicitly define expected columns by using
       ----oouuttppuutt _c_o_l_u_m_n_s_-_l_i_s_t in environments where a stable output is
       required.

       wwiippeeffss calls the BBLLKKRRRRPPAARRTT ioctl when it has erased a partition-table
       signature to inform the kernel about the change. The ioctl is called as
       the last step and when all specified signatures from all specified
       devices are already erased. This feature can be used to wipe content on
       partitions devices as well as partition table on a disk device, for
       example by wwiippeeffss --aa //ddeevv//ssddcc11 //ddeevv//ssddcc22 //ddeevv//ssddcc.

       Note that some filesystems and some partition tables store more magic
       strings on the device (e.g., FAT, ZFS, GPT). The wwiippeeffss command (since
       v2.31) lists all the offset where a magic strings have been detected.

       When option --aa is used, all magic strings that are visible for
       lliibbbbllkkiidd(3) are erased. In this case the wwiippeeffss scans the device again
       after each modification (erase) until no magic string is found.

       Note that by default wwiippeeffss does not erase nested partition tables on
       non-whole disk devices. For this the option ----ffoorrccee is required.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --aa, ----aallll
           Erase all available signatures. The set of erased signatures can be
           restricted with the --tt option.

       --bb, ----bbaacckkuupp
           Create a signature backup to the file
           _$_H_O_M_E_/_w_i_p_e_f_s_-_<_d_e_v_n_a_m_e_>_-_<_o_f_f_s_e_t_>_._b_a_k. For more details see the
           EEXXAAMMPPLLEE section.

       --ff, ----ffoorrccee
           Force erasure, even if the filesystem is mounted. This is required
           in order to erase a partition-table signature on a block device.

       --JJ, ----jjssoonn
           Use JSON output format.

       ----lloocckk[=_m_o_d_e]
           Use exclusive BSD lock for device or file it operates. The optional
           argument _m_o_d_e can be yyeess, nnoo (or 1 and 0) or nnoonnbblloocckk. If the _m_o_d_e
           argument is omitted, it defaults to ""yyeess"". This option overwrites
           environment variable $$LLOOCCKK__BBLLOOCCKK__DDEEVVIICCEE. The default is not to use
           any lock at all, but it’s recommended to avoid collisions with
           udevd or other tools.

       --ii, ----nnoohheeaaddiinnggss
           Do not print a header line.

       --OO, ----oouuttppuutt _l_i_s_t
           Specify which output columns to print. Use ----hheellpp to get a list of
           all supported columns.

       --nn, ----nnoo--aacctt
           Causes everything to be done except for the wwrriittee(2) call.

       --oo, ----ooffffsseett _o_f_f_s_e_t
           Specify the location (in bytes) of the signature which should be
           erased from the device. The _o_f_f_s_e_t number may include a "0x"
           prefix; then the number will be interpreted as a hex value. It is
           possible to specify multiple --oo options.

           The _o_f_f_s_e_t argument may be followed by the multiplicative suffixes
           KiB (=1024), MiB (=1024*1024), and so on for GiB, TiB, PiB, EiB,
           ZiB and YiB (the "iB" is optional, e.g., "K" has the same meaning
           as "KiB"), or the suffixes KB (=1000), MB (=1000*1000), and so on
           for GB, TB, PB, EB, ZB and YB.

       --pp, ----ppaarrssaabbllee
           Print out in parsable instead of printable format. Encode all
           potentially unsafe characters of a string to the corresponding hex
           value prefixed by '\x'.

       --qq, ----qquuiieett
           Suppress any messages after a successful signature wipe.

       --tt, ----ttyyppeess _l_i_s_t
           Limit the set of printed or erased signatures. More than one type
           may be specified in a comma-separated list. The list or individual
           types can be prefixed with 'no' to specify the types on which no
           action should be taken. For more details see mmoouunntt(8).

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit.

